 **`focus()` on a window that lives on another desktop switches to that desktop first**, so focus never lands on a window the user cannot see. `HOOKS.DESKTOP_SWITCHED` fires before the window's own `WINDOW_FOCUSED`. `open()` never does this: a window opened onto an inactive desktop (session restore, a lazily loaded native window) joins it without taking focus, and the active desktop stays where it is.
 
+#### Keep on top — Stable
+
+A window can be **kept on top**: it stays above every other window on the desktop, even while another window is focused or a new one opens. The user toggles it with the pin in the title bar (the built-in `core/always-on-top` control); code calls `setAlwaysOnTop()` and reads the window's `alwaysOnTop` property.
+
+```js
+const { windowManager } = wp.os;
+windowManager.setAlwaysOnTop( 'my-plugin-timer', true );
+windowManager.getById( 'my-plugin-timer' ).alwaysOnTop; // true
+```
+
+| Aspect | Behaviour |
+|---|---|
+| **Z-order** | Kept-on-top windows paint in a layer above the rest. Several can be on at once; among themselves they stack in normal focus order. Turning it off drops the window back into the normal layer at its place in focus order. Fullscreen still paints above everything. |
+| **Focus** | Unaffected. Focusing a window under a kept-on-top one gives it focus without bringing it in front; `getFocused()` still returns it. Toggling the flag moves neither focus nor the window. |
+| **Child windows** | A child paints in its owner's layer, so a child of a kept-on-top window is never hidden behind it. |
+| **Session** | Saved per window as `alwaysOnTop: true` and re-applied on reload. |
+| **Events** | `os-window-always-on-top-changed` and `HOOKS.WINDOW_ALWAYS_ON_TOP_CHANGED`, both `{ windowId, alwaysOnTop }`. Unknown ids and calls that change nothing fire neither. |
+
 #### Child windows — Stable
 
 A **child window** is a real window — own chrome, drag, resize, minimize, taskbar entry — with one rule layered on top: **its owner can never sit above it.** Clicking the owner hands focus to the child and shakes it rather than raising the owner.
